Black-Box Web Application Penetration Test

Engagement: GetJunoway Web Application
Assessment: Authorized Black-Box Web Application Penetration Test
Status: In Progress — approximately 45% complete
Environment: Kali Linux / Firefox
Focus: External attack surface, authentication, access control, security configuration, and public input handling

────────

Executive Summary

This case study documents an authorized black-box security assessment of the GetJunoway web application. Testing is being conducted from the perspective of an external attacker without source-code access and, for the current phase, without a functioning authenticated user account.

Assessment activity to date has covered public reconnaissance, technology identification, authentication-flow analysis, administrative-route validation, HTTP method behavior, security-header configuration, CORS behavior, error handling, client-side exposure, and public search-input handling.

The application was identified as a Next.js application with Supabase involved in authentication. An administrative route at /admin was discovered and validated. Although unauthenticated requests can reach the route and may receive an HTTP 200 response at the framework level, Next.js response data contains an application redirect to /login?next=/admin. No unauthorized administrative access has been demonstrated.

One security-hardening issue has been identified: the tested public response did not include a Content-Security-Policy header. Framework fingerprinting through X-Powered-By: Next.js was also observed as an informational exposure.

Public search functionality was tested for reflection, basic HTML injection behavior, special-character handling, malformed input, and unexpected parameter handling. No reflected XSS, HTML injection, database error disclosure, or privilege change was demonstrated in the tested cases.

Authenticated authorization testing remains pending because account creation/login is not currently available in the tested build.

