Assessment Activity

Phase 1 — Public Application Reconnaissance

Public application responses were collected and inspected using browser tools and command-line HTTP requests.

Reconnaissance identified:

• Next.js framework behavior
• Authentication-related application functionality
• /admin administrative route
• /login authentication route
• Public search functionality using /search?q=...
• Publicly delivered Next.js JavaScript chunks

Technology and route discovery were treated as reconnaissance observations only. No vulnerability was assigned solely because a technology or sensitive route was discoverable.

Result: Completed for the current assessment stage.

────────

Phase 2 — Authentication Flow Analysis

Authentication behavior was previously inspected using browser network traffic.

Invalid credentials produced a consistent rejection equivalent to:

```json
{
  "code": "invalid_credentials",
  "message": "Invalid login credentials"
}
```

A password-recovery workflow was not available in the tested build.

At the current stage, login/account creation is unavailable, preventing further authenticated session and account-boundary testing.

Result: Partial. No authentication bypass demonstrated. Authenticated testing remains pending.

────────

Phase 3 — Administrative Route Discovery & Validation

The following administrative route was identified:

```text
/admin
```

Direct unauthenticated requests were examined independently of browser rendering.

Example:

```bash
curl -s https://TARGET/admin -o /tmp/admin.html
```

Analysis of the returned Next.js data identified:

```text
NEXT_REDIRECT;replace;/login?next=/admin;307;
```

This showed that unauthenticated access is routed into the authentication workflow rather than being granted access to the administrative interface.

Validation Result

|Control Tested                                            |Result|
|----------------------------------------------------------|------|
|Administrative route externally discoverable              |Yes   |
|`/admin` reachable without an existing session            |Yes   |
|Administrative interface accessible without authentication|No    |
|Unauthenticated request routed to login                   |Yes   |
|Administrative authentication bypass confirmed            |No    |

Security disposition: No vulnerability issued for administrative-route exposure.

────────

Phase 4 — HTTP Method Handling

The /admin route was tested with multiple HTTP methods:

```text
GET
HEAD
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE
OPTIONS
```

All tested methods produced an HTTP 200 response at the application/framework level. However, testing did not demonstrate that alternate methods bypassed the authentication workflow or exposed protected administrative functionality.

This behavior is noteworthy because unsupported methods are commonly rejected explicitly, but status-code behavior alone does not establish a security vulnerability.

TRACE Validation

TRACE was separately tested:

```bash
curl -i -X TRACE https://TARGET/
```

Result:

```text
HTTP/2 405
405 Not Allowed
```

TRACE was rejected and the request was not reflected.

Result: No HTTP method-based authentication bypass demonstrated. TRACE protection passed.

