Phase 5 — Security Configuration Review

Security Headers Observed

The tested public response included protections such as:

```text
Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
```

Missing Content-Security-Policy

A direct header check was performed:

```bash
curl -sI https://TARGET | grep -i content-security-policy
```

No Content-Security-Policy header was returned in the tested response.

This does not demonstrate XSS by itself. CSP is a defense-in-depth browser control that can reduce the impact of certain content-injection vulnerabilities.

Framework Disclosure

The response exposed:

```text
X-Powered-By: Next.js
```

This provides minor technology fingerprinting information. No direct security impact has been demonstrated from the disclosure.

────────

Phase 6 — CORS Validation

A request containing an arbitrary external Origin header was sent:

```bash
curl -sI -H "Origin: https://evil.example" https://TARGET
```

The tested response did not reflect the supplied origin through Access-Control-Allow-Origin and did not expose an Access-Control-Allow-Credentials configuration that would indicate an obvious permissive CORS issue.

Result: No obvious arbitrary-origin CORS misconfiguration demonstrated on the tested endpoint.

────────

Phase 7 — Error Handling & Information Disclosure

A deliberately nonexistent route was requested to evaluate error handling.

The application returned:

```text
HTTP/2 404
X-Matched-Path: /_not-found
```

No visible stack trace, filesystem path, database exception, environment variable, source-code exception, or sensitive debugging information was observed.

Result: Error handling passed the tested information-disclosure check.

────────

Phase 8 — Client-Side Exposure Review

The login page was inspected for obvious API paths and public environment-variable names. Referenced Next.js JavaScript chunks were also identified and a representative chunk was downloaded for local inspection.

Testing did not identify obvious:

• /api/ endpoint disclosure in the inspected login HTML
• NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable names in the inspected page
• Authentication/backend URLs in the representative JavaScript chunk
• Supabase/Firebase/auth strings of security significance in that chunk

This was a limited client-side review and does not establish that no client-side endpoints or configuration exist elsewhere in the application.

Result: No obvious client-exposed secret or sensitive configuration identified in the tested material.

