Phase 9 — Public Search Input Testing

The public search feature uses a URL-controlled parameter:

```text
/search?q=test
```

Reflection Baseline

A unique marker was supplied:

```text
JUNO12345
```

The value was reflected in the search interface and results heading.

Reflection was confirmed, but reflection alone is not a vulnerability.

Basic HTML Injection Check

The following harmless markup was supplied:

```html
<b>JUNO12345</b>
```

The application displayed the markup literally rather than rendering JUNO12345 as injected HTML.

Result: Basic HTML injection not demonstrated.

Special-Character Handling

A string containing quotes, angle brackets, and an ampersand was submitted.

The characters remained visible as search text and did not visibly alter the page structure.

Result: No context breakout demonstrated in the tested reflection.

Malformed Input / Error Handling

A single quote was submitted as search input to check for obvious backend error leakage.

The application handled the input without exposing a visible SQL/database error, stack trace, or abnormal error page.

Result: No database-error disclosure demonstrated. This test alone does not establish immunity to injection.

Unexpected Parameter Handling

The following additional parameter was supplied:

```text
/search?q=test&admin=true
```

The application continued to behave as a normal search for test. No administrative interface, privileged data, error, or observable privilege change resulted.

Result: Unexpected parameter ignored in the tested case.

Search Security Disposition

Testing has not demonstrated:

• Reflected XSS
• Basic HTML injection
• Database error disclosure
• Privilege escalation through an unexpected URL parameter

Search testing is considered complete for this stage unless new behavior or attack surface is identified later.

