Findings Status

Finding 01 — Missing Content-Security-Policy Header

Severity: Low / Security Hardening
Affected Component: Public web responses
Status: Confirmed configuration observation

Description

The tested public response did not include a Content-Security-Policy HTTP response header.

Security Impact

The absence of CSP does not create an XSS vulnerability by itself. However, CSP provides defense in depth by restricting the sources and execution contexts available to browser content. If a separate content-injection vulnerability were introduced, the absence of an effective CSP could increase its potential impact.

Reproduction

```bash
curl -sI https://TARGET | grep -i content-security-policy
```

No header was returned.

Recommendation

Implement and test an application-specific Content Security Policy. Begin with a policy compatible with the application’s required scripts, styles, images, connections, frames, and other resources, then progressively tighten it. Avoid deploying an overly broad policy solely to satisfy the presence of the header.

────────

Informational Observation — Framework Fingerprinting

Severity: Informational

Responses disclose:

```text
X-Powered-By: Next.js
```

Removing unnecessary framework-identification headers can slightly reduce passive technology fingerprinting, although this should not be treated as a substitute for patching or secure application design.

